## 0%-Scored Objectives (11 objectives, 31 questions)

### Objective 1 — Session resumption: targeted re-analysis + context injection (0%) — Domain 1

**Q1.** A developer resumes a named multi-hour session after a crash. Three of the twelve files
the session had already analyzed were modified by a teammate's merge while the session was down.
The developer wants to resume without repeating the full twelve-file analysis.

A) Resume the session as-is with no additional instruction, since resumption automatically
   re-validates file state against the working directory before continuing.
B) Start an entirely new session and paste in a written summary of the prior twelve-file
   analysis, since a merge during downtime invalidates the value of resuming at all.
C) Resume the session and explicitly tell the agent which three files changed since the crash,
   so it can target its re-analysis to just those and treat the other nine as still valid.
D) Resume the session and instruct the agent to re-read all twelve files from scratch before
   continuing, since any file could theoretically have drifted from the merge.

**Q2.** A session investigating a bug across many files crashes. On resume, the team debates
resuming with full prior history versus starting fresh with an injected summary. Two files
central to the investigation were rewritten by another engineer during the outage; the rest of
the codebase is untouched.

A) Resume the session, but explicitly flag the two rewritten files for targeted re-analysis,
   leaving the rest of the restored context intact.
B) Start a new session with an injected summary of prior findings, since any file-level change
   during downtime should be treated as invalidating the whole investigation's assumptions.
C) Start a new session with no injected context at all, relying on the fresh instance to
   re-derive everything the crashed session had already found.
D) Resume the session and re-run the entire investigation's tool calls from the beginning, since
   injected summaries can never substitute for live-verified state.

**Q3.** A resumed session correctly restores its full prior conversation and tool-call history.
The agent then edits a function whose signature was changed by a teammate after the crash —
information the resumed session has no way of knowing unless told.

A) This indicates resumption itself failed, since a correctly working resume mechanism should
   automatically detect any external file changes made during the interruption.
B) This is resolved by increasing the model's context window, since a larger window would let the
   agent infer the signature change from surrounding code during editing.
C) This is resolved by restarting from a completely fresh session with no context at all, since
   only a blank-slate session can be trusted around any externally-changed file.
D) This is an inherent limitation of resumption: informing the agent of the specific external
   change is necessary, since resumption restores prior state, not omniscience about changes made
   outside it.

### Objective 2 — Selecting the appropriate review architecture: plan mode vs. direct execution vs. multi-phase (0%) — Domain 3

**Q4.** A team is deciding how a review agent should operate across three request types: (a) a
one-line typo fix in a comment, (b) a cross-cutting rename affecting 40 files with no behavior
change, and (c) a schema migration touching application and database layers with rollback risk.

A) Use plan mode uniformly for all three, since presenting a plan before any change is always the
   safer default regardless of how mechanical or reversible the task is.
B) Use direct execution for (a), a lightweight multi-phase workflow for (b), and plan mode for
   (c), matching each task's actual risk and ambiguity.
C) Use a multi-phase workflow uniformly for all three, since staged checkpoints are strictly more
   thorough than either plan mode or direct execution alone.
D) Use direct execution uniformly for all three, since plan mode's overhead is never justified
   once an experienced reviewer approves pull requests downstream.

**Q5.** A refactoring agent migrates a payment-processing module to a new SDK version. There's one
clear, unambiguous target API, but the change touches 30 files of financial-transaction logic with
no automated rollback.

A) Direct execution is warranted, since plan mode's value is tied strictly to ambiguity about the
   desired outcome, which this task doesn't have.
B) Direct execution is warranted, since the 30-file scope is better handled by increasing model
   capability than by adding a review step.
C) Plan mode is warranted here — not because the target is ambiguous, but because of the size and
   hard-to-reverse risk profile; direct execution should follow once the plan is approved.
D) A multi-phase workflow with no human checkpoint is warranted, since breaking the migration into
   phases removes the need for plan mode's approval step entirely.

**Q6.** A team's CI pipeline runs a fully automated review on every pull request, with no human
present during the run, gating downstream deployment on pass/fail.

A) Direct execution fits here, since plan mode's approval step assumes a human present to review
   and approve the plan — unavailable in an unattended run — with structured output serving as the
   gate instead.
B) Plan mode fits here, since presenting a plan before any review activity is always required for
   a process that gates deployment, human present or not.
C) A multi-phase workflow with a plan-mode checkpoint between each phase fits here, since
   deployment-gating processes always warrant the heaviest available review architecture.
D) Direct execution fits here, but only because CI pipelines are inherently lower-risk than local
   development work.

### Objective 3 — State persistence for multi-agent pipeline resumption (0%) — Domain 1

**Q7.** A five-stage document pipeline (extract → classify → validate → enrich → publish)
processes 10,000 documents. It crashes after stage 3 has completed for 6,200 documents. The team
wants to resume without re-running completed stages for those 6,200 or losing their results.

A) Persist only a single running counter of total documents fully processed, since knowing how
   many are done is sufficient to determine where to resume the batch.
B) Persist the final stage-5 output only for documents that reached it, and re-run stages 1
   through 5 in full for every document that hadn't yet reached stage 5.
C) Re-run all five stages for all 10,000 documents on resume, accepting the redundant cost, since
   partial multi-stage state can't be trusted after an unplanned crash.
D) Persist each document's per-stage completion status and output to durable storage as each
   stage finishes, so resume reads that state and continues only the remaining stages per document.

**Q8.** Two agents in a pipeline — extraction and validation — both write completion state to the
same per-document record with no coordination. A race condition sometimes lets validation's write
overwrite extraction's findings for a document both touch in quick succession.

A) This is a persisted-state design gap, not an argument against persistence itself — the schema
   needs structure (separate fields per agent, or ordered/versioned writes) preventing one agent's
   write from clobbering another's.
B) This is resolved by having only the validation agent persist state going forward, since
   validation's output supersedes extraction's regardless of timing.
C) This is resolved by removing state persistence for this pipeline entirely, since any two-agent
   write contention proves persistence isn't safe for pipelines of this shape.
D) This is expected and acceptable — some data loss on concurrent writes is an inherent,
   unavoidable cost of any multi-agent state-persistence scheme.

**Q9.** A crash-recovery design has each pipeline stage export a state file, and a coordinator load
a manifest of these files on resume. After a crash, the manifest shows stages 1 and 2 completed
for a document, but stage 3 is simply absent from the manifest — no entry exists either way.

A) "Absent" always safely means "not started," so the coordinator should restart stage 3 for this
   document without further checks in every case.
B) "Absent" indicates a corrupted state file, so the fix is switching from a manifest-based design
   to relying solely on raw conversation/session history for recovery.
C) "Absent" is ambiguous between "never started" and "started but interrupted mid-write," and the
   coordinator needs to distinguish these before restarting, since redoing a stage with side
   effects mid-flight isn't the same as redoing one that never began.
D) "Absent" is not actionable information at all, so the pipeline should discard the manifest and
   reprocess the document from stage 1 regardless of what completed.

### Objective 4 — Multi-agent orchestration pattern selection: coordinator-worker, parallel, sequential (0%) — Domain 1

**Q10.** A research task requires summarizing five independent, unrelated papers with no
dependency between them, followed by one synthesis step needing all five summaries together.

A) Dispatch the five summarization subagents in parallel within a single coordinator turn, then
   run synthesis once all five have returned, since the five subtasks are genuinely independent.
B) Dispatch the five summarization subagents in parallel, and also dispatch synthesis in parallel
   with them in the same turn, since parallel dispatch broadly applies to every step here.
C) Use one subagent to read and summarize all five papers within a single invocation, then a
   second subagent to synthesize, since fewer subagent calls always reduces coordination overhead.
D) Process the five papers strictly sequentially, one subagent invocation per turn, then
   synthesize, since sequential processing offers stronger reliability guarantees for any
   multi-source task.

**Q11.** A due-diligence pipeline needs a legal check, a financial check, and a reputational check
on the same company — none depending on the others' output — followed by one final report
combining all three.

A) Coordinator runs the three checks as a strict sequential pipeline, each waiting on the previous
   one's completion, since findings should always be assembled in a fixed order for auditability.
B) Coordinator delegates all four steps, including the final report, to a single subagent in one
   invocation, since consolidating the entire workflow is the most latency-efficient structure.
C) Coordinator dispatches the three checks in parallel, and does not run a separate report step at
   all, since a well-instructed reputational subagent can synthesize using its own findings alone.
D) Coordinator dispatches the three checks in parallel in one turn, then runs the report once all
   three return, since the checks are mutually independent and only the report depends on them.

**Q12.** A support-ticket system needs step 1 (classify urgency) to complete before step 2 (route
to the correct specialist queue) can run, since routing logic depends on the classification's
output.

A) This fits a coordinator-worker pattern with no ordering constraint at all — let both subagents
   run freely and reconcile any resulting inconsistency afterward.
B) This fits a sequential pipeline — step 2 genuinely depends on step 1's output, so parallel
   dispatch of both in the same turn would let step 2 run without the result it requires.
C) This fits a sequential pipeline, but only because two-step workflows should always default to
   sequential processing rather than parallel, independent of whether the steps actually depend on
   each other.
D) This fits a parallel-execution pattern — dispatching classification and routing in the same
   turn is always preferable when only two steps are involved.

### Objective 5 — Claude Code review configuration: correct standards + tool restriction + structured output (0%) — Domain 3

**Q13.** A team's automated PR-review agent correctly loads project `CLAUDE.md` conventions and
produces well-formatted JSON findings, but is also given unrestricted `allowed-tools`, including
full `Bash` and `Write` access to any file in the repo.

A) This configuration is acceptable as-is, since correctly loaded standards and correctly
   structured output are the two properties that actually determine review quality.
B) This configuration is acceptable as-is, since a review task is inherently read-only in effect
   regardless of which tools are technically reachable by the agent performing it.
C) The tool access should be restricted to read/analysis-appropriate tools, since an unrestricted
   toolset creates avoidable risk of unintended modification for a task that doesn't need it.
D) The tool access should be restricted, but only because unrestricted `Bash` slows down review
   latency, not because of any risk related to unintended modification.

**Q14.** A review configuration correctly restricts tool access to read-only tools and produces
clean structured JSON, but is pointed at a stale `CLAUDE.md` copied from a different, unrelated
project, containing outdated conventions.

A) This configuration is acceptable as-is, since tool restriction is the more consequential of the
   two properties, and the specific conventions file matters less once destructive access is
   removed.
B) Both correctly-scoped tool access and correctly-loaded standards are independently necessary;
   scoped tools alone don't compensate for reviewing against the wrong conventions.
C) This configuration is acceptable as-is, provided the stale `CLAUDE.md` is at least syntactically
   well-formed markdown, since malformed files are the actual failure mode being guarded against.
D) The fix is to abandon `CLAUDE.md` entirely and rely only on the structured JSON output schema to
   convey project standards to the review.

**Q15.** A review configuration restricts tools correctly and loads the correct, current
`CLAUDE.md`, but produces its findings as free-form prose rather than the structured JSON the
downstream PR-commenting script expects, causing the automation to silently drop most findings.

A) This is acceptable as-is, since correctly-scoped tools and correctly-loaded standards are the
   two properties that most determine whether a review's findings are substantively accurate.
B) This is fixed by adding more detail to `CLAUDE.md` describing desired severity levels, since
   richer conventions naturally produce more consistently structured output.
C) This is fixed by giving the review agent write access so it can post PR comments directly,
   bypassing the downstream script's structured-input requirement.
D) Structured output needs to be explicitly configured (e.g., `--output-format json` with a
   schema, or a forced tool call) — correct standards and tool scoping don't by themselves
   guarantee the output shape a downstream consumer requires.

### Objective 6 — Automated test-generation quality: existing tests + fixtures + meaningful-vs-trivial criteria (0%) — Domain 3

**Q16.** An agent asked to "add tests for the new refund-processing function" is given no existing
test files as context and no fixture conventions. It generates ten new tests, several duplicating
an existing (unshown) suite, and most only asserting the function doesn't throw an exception.

A) Provide the existing test files as context and explicit criteria distinguishing meaningful
   assertions from trivial ones, rather than leaving both gaps unaddressed.
B) Provide only the existing test files as context, since seeing prior coverage is sufficient on
   its own to also raise the substantive quality of each individual test's assertions.
C) Increase the number of requested tests from ten to thirty, since a larger volume of tests
   compensates for both the duplication and the shallow-assertion problems described.
D) Instruct the agent via a one-line prompt addition to "write more thorough tests," since a
   stronger directive alone reliably raises assertion quality without supplying either context.

**Q17.** A team supplies an agent with existing test files and clear fixture conventions, but
never defines what counts as a "meaningful" test versus a trivial one. The agent still generates
several tests that only check a function returns without error.

A) This is already resolved by the existing test files and fixture conventions supplied, since
   seeing real prior test structure is functionally equivalent to an explicit standard.
B) The team needs to add explicit criteria for what constitutes a meaningful behavioral test (e.g.,
   asserting specific expected outputs for given inputs) versus a trivial one — the other two
   inputs don't communicate that distinction.
C) This is resolved by removing fixture conventions from the context entirely, since fixtures are
   unrelated to whether an individual test's assertions are substantive.
D) This is resolved by asking the agent to generate fewer tests overall, since a smaller requested
   count naturally biases the agent toward writing only its most meaningful assertions first.

### Objective 7 — Selecting the correct configuration mechanism: CLAUDE.md, .claude/rules/, Skills, hooks, settings (0%) — Domain 3

**Q18.** A team needs four things configured: (1) a standing coding-style convention that should
always apply project-wide, (2) a convention that should apply only to files matching
`**/*.test.ts` regardless of directory, (3) a deterministic, unconditional block on `rm -rf` ever
executing, and (4) a bundled reference workflow invoked on request for "run our release checklist."

A) (1) `CLAUDE.md`, (2) a `.claude/rules/` file with a glob `paths` pattern, (3) a hook, (4) a
   Skill — matching each need's scope and enforcement requirement to its purpose-built mechanism.
B) (1) and (2) should both be `CLAUDE.md` sections, since combining all standing conventions into
   one file is simpler than splitting by scope; (3) and (4) should both be Skills.
C) All four should be `.claude/rules/` files with different glob patterns, since sufficiently
   broad patterns can substitute for `CLAUDE.md`, hooks, and Skills alike.
D) All four should be hooks, since hooks provide the strongest enforcement guarantee and are
   therefore the safest default choice regardless of the actual configuration need.

**Q19.** A `.claude/rules/` file is configured with `paths: ["src/legacy/**"]`, and a team
describes this as "using glob-pattern rules to keep a convention scoped by file type across
scattered directories."

A) This description is accurate, since any `.claude/rules/` file is, by definition, scoping by
   file type rather than by directory regardless of the actual glob pattern used.
B) This description is inaccurate, but only because `.claude/rules/` files can never be used for
   anything under a `src/` directory specifically.
C) This description is accurate, but only because the rule also needs an `argument-hint` set to
   properly scope by file type.
D) This description is inaccurate for this specific rule — this path scopes by directory location,
   not file type; the glob-pattern advantage over `CLAUDE.md` shows up for a pattern like
   `**/*.test.ts` spanning many directories, not one.

**Q20.** A team wants a commit workflow to always require a JIRA ticket ID in every commit
message, with no exceptions even under time pressure — and separately wants a personal shortcut
reformatting commit messages to one developer's preferred style, useful across every repo.

A) Both needs call for the same mechanism — a project-scoped Skill — since Skills can express both
   a strict commit requirement and a personal formatting preference equally well.
B) The JIRA-ticket requirement calls for a hook that deterministically blocks a commit lacking a
   matching ID; the personal shortcut calls for a user-scoped command, following the developer
   across projects.
C) The JIRA-ticket requirement calls for a project-scoped `CLAUDE.md` instruction stating the
   rule, since documenting it in project config is sufficient to guarantee "no exceptions."
D) The personal shortcut calls for a project-scoped command in the repo's `.claude/commands/`,
   since scoping it to the project ensures it's available exactly where needed most.

### Objective 8 — Subagent output schema design: structured data, prose, and citation metadata (0%) — Domain 1

**Q21.** A document-analysis subagent returns findings as one narrative paragraph mixing two
claims and their sources inline. Synthesis later can't reliably attribute which number came from
which source or page.

A) The subagent's output should stay as prose, but should mention each source and page number more
   frequently, since repetition within prose is sufficient to preserve attribution.
B) The synthesis step should be responsible for re-deriving correct attribution by re-reading both
   original source documents, since restructuring the subagent's own output shifts complexity to
   the wrong layer.
C) The subagent's output schema should separate each finding into distinct fields — claim text,
   source identifier, and location — rather than embedding references inline within one paragraph.
D) The subagent should omit page-level detail entirely and cite only document-level sources, since
   page numbers add complexity without adding meaningful attribution value.

**Q22.** A subagent designs its own output schema for a task combining (a) structured numerical
findings, (b) prose narrative explaining nuance, and (c) citation metadata per claim. It returns
everything as one long, undifferentiated text block.

A) The output schema should render each content type in the form best suited to it — structured
   fields for numbers, dedicated prose for nuance, separate citation fields per claim — rather than
   collapsing all three together.
B) The output schema should drop the prose narrative entirely and return only structured numerical
   findings with citations, since nuance isn't something a subagent's schema needs to preserve.
C) The output schema should convert everything, including prose and citations, into numerical
   fields only, since a fully numerical schema is the most machine-parseable format available.
D) The output schema is fine as one unstructured text block, since downstream synthesis can always
   parse whatever natural-language structure the subagent chooses to use.

**Q23.** A subagent's output schema correctly separates structured data, prose, and citation
fields — but the citation field stores only a document title, with no page, section, or excerpt,
making it hard for a reviewer to verify a claim quickly.

A) This is already sufficient, since separating citation metadata into its own field, regardless
   of what it contains, is the property that matters for downstream synthesis.
B) This is resolved by moving the citation field back into the prose narrative, since embedding a
   title within the narrative gives equivalent verification ability to a separate field.
C) This is resolved by removing the citation field entirely and requiring the coordinator to
   re-locate the correct source and page for every claim during review.
D) The citation metadata should be extended to include enough locating detail (page/section plus a
   short excerpt) alongside the title, since a bare title alone doesn't let a reviewer efficiently
   verify a specific claim.

### Objective 9 — Synthesis agent behavior: preserving source-level uncertainty and contested claims (0%) — Domain 5

**Q24.** A synthesis agent receives two credible but conflicting figures for the same statistic —
one source says 12%, another says 18% in the same year. It reports only 18%, with no indication a
second value existed.

A) The synthesis agent correctly resolved the conflict by choosing the more recent-sounding
   source, since presenting a single confident figure is what a synthesis step is for.
B) Both figures should be preserved with source attribution, explicitly flagged as contested,
   rather than the agent silently resolving the conflict by presenting only one as settled fact.
C) The lower of the two figures should always be reported as the more conservative, and therefore
   safer, estimate whenever sources conflict.
D) Neither figure should be reported at all, since any conflict between two credible sources makes
   the statistic entirely unusable for a final report.

**Q25.** A synthesis agent combines a claim confirmed independently by three sources with a claim
traceable to a single blog reference. The final report presents both at identical apparent
confidence, with no distinguishing language.

A) A generic disclaimer should be appended once at the very end of the report noting "some
   findings may be less certain," rather than distinguishing confidence at the individual level.
B) The single-sourced claim should be removed from the report entirely, since a synthesis report
   should include only claims independently confirmed by multiple sources.
C) The report should distinguish the well-established, multiply-sourced claim from the tentative,
   single-sourced one, rather than presenting both at the same apparent confidence.
D) This is acceptable as-is, since a synthesis agent's role is to combine findings into a report,
   not to evaluate the relative reliability of each finding's underlying sources.

**Q26.** A synthesis agent is given a finding with an explicit publication date and a second, more
recent finding on the same metric with a different value. It flags this as a "data inconsistency
requiring investigation" rather than recognizing the metric may simply have changed over time.

A) Requiring and using publication/collection dates lets synthesis distinguish a genuine
   contradiction from a legitimate temporal difference, which raw values alone can't tell apart.
B) This is correctly flagged, since any two different values for the same metric should always be
   treated as a genuine data-quality problem needing investigation.
C) The fix is to always trust whichever finding has the more recent retrieval timestamp and
   silently discard the older one, without flagging anything to the reader.
D) The fix is to remove numerical figures from synthesis output entirely, since any metric that can
   change over time is inherently unsuitable for a synthesis report.

### Objective 10 — Reducing automated review false positives via persistent project-specific context (0%) — Domain 4

**Q27.** An automated reviewer repeatedly flags a specific null-check pattern as a bug, even though
it is a deliberate, well-known, team-specific convention. The team's fix attempt: instruct the
reviewer to "only report issues you are highly confident about."

A) The confidence-based instruction is sufficient on its own, since a reviewer told to report only
   high-confidence issues will naturally learn which patterns turn out to be false positives.
B) The false-positive pattern should be handled by permanently disabling the review category
   responsible for null-check findings, discarding that category's genuine value along with it.
C) The false-positive pattern should be handled by increasing review frequency, since running the
   same check more often will eventually average out to fewer false positives per PR.
D) Supply the specific accepted pattern directly as persistent context so the reviewer recognizes
   this exact pattern as intentional, rather than relying on a generic confidence instruction.

**Q28.** A team supplies accepted-pattern examples to reduce false positives, but every example is
drawn from the API layer, while the actual false positives occur in the database layer's own
distinct, unrelated conventions.

A) The examples need to be representative of where the false positives actually occur; accepted
   patterns are often subsystem-specific, so API-layer examples won't transfer to the database
   layer's own conventions.
B) This is sufficient as-is, since accepted patterns supplied from one part of a codebase
   generalize automatically to every other part regardless of subsystem.
C) The fix is to remove all supplied examples and rely solely on the reviewer's own default
   judgment across every subsystem.
D) The fix is to supply more API-layer examples, on the theory that a larger volume of the same
   examples will eventually generalize to the unrelated database-layer conventions.

### Objective 11 — tool_choice to guarantee invocation + sequencing prerequisite data before dependent tools (0%) — Domain 4

**Q29.** An extraction pipeline needs the model to *always* emit a `tool_use` call for
`extract_invoice_fields` — never plain text — regardless of how unusual the document's formatting
is, since downstream code expects a `tool_use` block on every call.

A) Set `tool_choice` to `"auto"`, since "auto" maximizes the model's flexibility to choose the best
   response format for each document's particular formatting.
B) Increase the model's context window, since a document is more likely to trigger a genuine tool
   call when more surrounding context is available.
C) Force the specific tool via `tool_choice`, guaranteeing a `tool_use` block every time, and
   design the schema so an unparseable document can still be represented honestly (e.g., nullable
   fields) instead of the model escaping into prose.
D) Leave `tool_choice` unset and rely on a strongly worded system-prompt instruction insisting the
   tool always be called, since sufficiently emphatic prose achieves the same guarantee.

**Q30.** A workflow needs `get_customer_account_id` to run and return a valid ID before
`process_refund` can be called. Currently, the model sometimes attempts `process_refund` with a
placeholder ID when it hasn't yet called the lookup tool.

A) Sequence the workflow so the prerequisite tool's successful output is required before the
   dependent tool is deterministically permitted, at the harness/hook level — not left to the
   model's own reasoning about the dependency.
B) Merge `get_customer_account_id` and `process_refund` into a single combined tool call, since
   consolidating both operations removes any need to sequence them at all.
C) Add a system-prompt instruction telling the model "always call get_customer_account_id before
   process_refund," relying on the model's own judgment to honor the ordering.
D) Set `tool_choice` to `"any"` for the entire conversation, since "any" guarantees that some tool
   is called at every turn, which by itself enforces the required calling order.

**Q31.** A document-classification pipeline has three possible extraction schemas depending on
document type, which isn't known in advance. The team wants some extraction tool guaranteed to be
called every time, without pre-committing to one specific schema by name.

A) `tool_choice` forcing one specific named schema tool guarantees the strongest compliance, since
   forcing a named tool is always the more reliable choice whenever guaranteed invocation matters.
B) No `tool_choice` configuration is needed here at all; leaving it unset while providing detailed
   prose descriptions of all three schemas is functionally equivalent to any setting.
C) `tool_choice: "auto"` is correct here, since "auto" is the most general-purpose option and
   general-purpose settings are the safest default whenever multiple valid tools exist.
D) `tool_choice: "any"` guarantees a tool call happens every turn while still letting the model
   select which of the three schema-specific tools actually fits the document.

