## 50%-Scored Objectives (6 objectives, 17 questions)

### Objective 12 — Goal-oriented vs. procedural subagent delegation, maintaining coordinator visibility (50%) — Domain 1

**Q32.** A coordinator delegates to a document-analysis subagent with rigid, step-by-step
instructions ("open page 1, extract data, then page 2, then page 3, in that exact order"). A
document with an unusual page structure causes the subagent to fail rigidly.

A) The procedural instruction is the correct level of detail here, since more granular,
   step-locked instructions always reduce a subagent's error rate regardless of document variance.
B) A goal-oriented instruction ("extract and summarize the key findings, regardless of structure")
   lets the subagent adapt to unusual structures a fixed sequence cannot accommodate.
C) The fix is to add even more procedural detail specifying a distinct branch for every possible
   page-order variation the team can anticipate in advance.
D) The fix is to remove the subagent entirely and have the coordinator process every document
   directly, since delegation is the source of the rigidity being observed.

**Q33.** A coordinator gives subagents purely goal-oriented instructions with no reporting
mechanism at all — subagents return only a final summary, with no visibility into which sources
were checked or what approach was taken.

A) The fix is switching back to fully procedural, step-by-step instructions, since only procedural
   delegation is compatible with the coordinator retaining any visibility into subagent behavior.
B) This is the correct and necessary tradeoff — goal-oriented delegation inherently sacrifices
   coordinator visibility in exchange for the subagent's adaptability.
C) Goal-oriented instructions and coordinator visibility are separate concerns — the coordinator
   can still require subagents to report what they did alongside their goal-oriented output.
D) The fix is to have the coordinator independently re-derive what each subagent did by repeating
   the same search/analysis work itself after each subagent returns.

**Q34.** A subagent given the goal-oriented instruction "assess whether this codebase follows
secure coding practices" interprets "secure" idiosyncratically, flagging minor stylistic choices
while missing an actual SQL injection vulnerability.

A) The goal itself needs sharper, more operational criteria for what specifically counts as a
   security issue versus a style issue, rather than abandoning goal-oriented delegation entirely.
B) This outcome proves goal-oriented instructions are inherently unreliable for any
   codebase-assessment task, and procedural, step-locked instructions should always be used
   instead.
C) The fix is removing all task framing entirely and letting the subagent decide, with no goal
   statement at all, what to look for in the codebase.
D) The fix is running the identical subagent invocation five separate times and averaging or
   voting across the five sets of findings to smooth out the inconsistency.

### Objective 13 — Selecting API processing mode: synchronous vs. Batches (50%) — Domain 4

**Q35.** A team runs a blocking pre-merge CI check and a technical-debt report generated overnight
